{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically above realized prices, especially beyond ~day 8–10; apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: when the market is in a downtrend, the model underestimates the speed and depth of declines (predicts stabilization/rebound too early); increase negative slope during falling regimes.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: the model projects stronger/longer recoveries than realized after short-term upticks; dampen rebound amplitude and shorten rebound duration.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: near-term (first ~5–7 days) errors are small, but mid/long-horizon errors expand materially; use a horizon-weighted shrinkage toward a lower path (or toward recent trend) for days 10–30.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model mean-reverts upward too aggressively (pulls prices back toward ~70–75) while realized series continues drifting lower; reduce upward mean-reversion strength and allow lower equilibrium during bearish periods."
  ]
}
